# gemma4:vlm — the summarizer half of timmy's OpenViking/editor pair (IDEA-1105).
#
# OpenViking's `vlm` config slot is not one prompt. It drives, for a
# compendium-shaped corpus:
#
#   semantic.document_summary     documentation files   60-180 words, plain text
#   semantic.file_summary         other non-code files  50-150 words, plain text
#   semantic.code_summary         code files            80-200 words, plain text
#   semantic.overview_generation  directory L1 overview 400-800 words, structured
#   vision.*  (7 templates)       images, pages, tables JSON, requires an image
#
# Measured 2026-09-18 against those exact templates, pulled from the running
# openviking pod, on the same corpus (deepseek-coder-v2:instruct for contrast):
#
#   role                deepseek                     this tag
#   code_summary        1/6 in band, markdown 5/6    6/6 in band, 0 markdown
#   file_summary        2/6 in band, markdown 3/6    6/6 in band, 0 markdown
#   document_summary    1/7 in band, markdown 5/7    7/7 in band, 0 markdown
#   vision.*            refused (not multimodal)     accepted and described
#
# The short-form gap is instruction-following, not comprehension: deepseek is a
# CODE model being asked to summarise documents, and no prompt-level remedy fixed
# it (a system instruction to obey the contract made it WORSE, 0/7; a corrective
# retry naming the exact violation still reached only 2/7).
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION, and it is OpenViking's, not this model's:
# semantic.overview_generation asks for 400-800 words PLUS one H3 subsection per
# entry, while storage caps the result at semantic.overview_max_chars.
# A compliant 800-word overview is already ~5200 chars. Both models lose most of
# their overview to _enforce_size_limits -- this tag keeps 36% of its output and
# 9 of 14 resolving links. Raising overview_max_chars or shrinking the template's
# target is the fix; changing models is not.
#
# RESOLVED 2026-09-18/21 (BUG-1155): overview_max_chars was raised 4000 -> 12000
# and the overview_generation template was overridden per-entry (homelab #136)
# with a terminal-period rule and a 5-word gloss cap (#139), because the flat
# nav list had no sentence boundaries and _truncate_generated_text discarded the
# WHOLE list once it passed the cap. bugs/dipdash went 458 chars / 0 targets to
# 11851 chars / 105 targets. The paragraph above is kept as the record of why
# those keys moved; do not re-derive it from the stale 4000 figure.
#
# TWO OPERATIONAL FACTS THIS TAG DEPENDS ON:
#  1. gemma4 is a THINKING model. Without `think: false` it spends the whole
#     token budget in a `thinking` field and returns EMPTY content. OV reaches it
#     through chat-ollama-proxy, whose INJECT_REASONING_NONE=true supplies that
#     suppression -- so the proxy is LOAD-BEARING on this path, not optional.
#  2. On /api/generate gemma4 streams nothing until completion (ERR-1004
#     family). Drive it through /api/chat. OV is provider "openai" and calls
#     /v1/chat/completions, so it is on the safe path -- keep it there.
#
# Recipe is the retired agentpair:agent-gemma4-12b unchanged: its sampling is
# gemma4's single published row and needs no adjustment for this workload.
#
# Requires: ollama pull gemma4:12b-it-qat
# Build:    ollama create gemma4:vlm -f gemma4-vlm.Modelfile

FROM gemma4:12b-it-qat

# RENDERER / PARSER inherited (gemma4) — never restated. `think` is per-request.

# 32768, raised from 16384 on 2026-09-21 after the BUG-1155 cold ingest.
#
# THE OVERFLOW IS DEMONSTRATED. During the 2026-09-21 cold ingest the ollama
# daemon logged 6,221 llama.cpp slot releases, of which 36 carry `truncated = 1`
# and EVERY ONE of those sits at exactly `n_tokens = 16383` -- num_ctx minus
# one, the context wall. The highest non-truncated release is 16368, and 44
# releases exceed 15,000 tokens, so this is a population pressing against the
# ceiling rather than one outlier. Window: 02:06Z to 08:28Z.
#
#   journalctl -u ollama | grep -o "n_tokens = [0-9]*, truncated = [0-9]*"
#
# IT IS A GENERATION-CAPACITY STOP, AND CONTENT WAS LOST. Review raised the
# right objection: `truncated = 1` alone does not prove loss, because an EOS
# arriving at the boundary sets STOP_TYPE_EOS while leaving the flag set
# (b10864 tools/server/server-context.cpp:1952). The journal records no stop
# reason at all -- `stop type`, `stopped_eos` and `stopped_limit` are absent --
# so the flag cannot settle it. Joining the release lines to
# `slot print_timing` on task id does:
#
#   50 truncated tasks with both counts (12h window)
#   prompt tokens       8,474 - 13,666     <- all WELL under 16384
#   generated tokens    2,717 -  7,909
#   prompt + generated  16,378 - 16,383, mean 16,383
#   exactly 16,383      44 of 50
#
# The prompts are nowhere near the limit; the TOTAL is pinned at the ceiling
# across prompts that vary by 5,000 tokens. Natural EOS would scatter those
# totals the way the 7,055 untruncated releases scatter (p50 1,496, p99 9,344,
# max 16,378). Forty-four exact landings on n_ctx-1 is a hard limit, not a
# coincidence. This also resolves the prompt-vs-generation question the earlier
# comment hedged on: prompt assembly was never the issue -- generation ran into
# the wall, exactly as the non-shifting capacity path predicts.
#
# Nothing surfaced upstream: OpenViking's text-completion path returns message
# content and drops finish_reason (openviking/models/vlm/backends/openai_vlm.py,
# models/vlm/base.py), so a length-limited response never becomes an error.
# That is a source fact; the zero finish_reason lines in the log prove nothing
# on their own, and neither do the zero "truncating input" matches -- that
# string exists in v0.34.1, but its warning path is only one of several and the
# chat path logs truncation at Debug level (ollama server/prompt.go).
#
# ATTRIBUTION IS BY EXCLUSION AND CONDITIONAL. Ollama launches a runner with
# NumCtx x numParallel and llama.cpp divides it per sequence, so the 8K FIM
# runner cannot produce a 16,383-token sequence -- these are the 16K runner's.
# But the journal retains no runner-start or n_ctx lines (both models have been
# pinned since before the window), so "only gemma4:vlm and qwen2.5-coder:fim
# were live" is taken from the deployment's steady state, not proven from this
# log. Nor does it prove every caller was OpenViking. And 50/7,105 is a
# host-wide slot-release fraction, not an OpenViking failure rate.
#
# AN EARLIER VERSION OF THIS COMMENT GOT BOTH DIRECTIONS WRONG. First it
# asserted the overflow from "60000 + 12000 chars ~= 18000 tokens", which is not
# a context requirement at all: max_overview_prompt_chars bounds SUMMARY STRINGS
# before the template is added, overview_max_chars truncates the STORED overview
# AFTER generation and link expansion without capping completion tokens
# (max_tokens is unset), and 4 chars/token was an estimate presented as a
# measurement. Review then correctly refuted that reasoning, and the comment
# over-corrected to "headroom, no overflow observed" -- while the evidence sat
# unread in the daemon's own journal. The lesson is the reading, not the
# arithmetic: measure the thing, do not derive it from config keys.
#
# WHY THE RAISE IS AFFORDABLE, with the corrected arithmetic:
#
#   40 of 48 layers   sliding window 1024, kv_heads 8, k/v len 256
#    8 of 48 layers   full attention,      kv_heads 1, k/v len 512
#
# q8_0 stores 34 bytes per 32 values, so full-attention KV is 8.5 KiB/token/
# slot (not 8.0), and the pinned allocator pads SWA cells by n_ubatch (512)
# plus 256-cell rounding -- 1536 cells per slot, not a flat 1024
# (llama.cpp b10864 src/llama-kv-cache-iswa.cpp:69).
#
#   num_ctx 16384 x 4 slots -> ~1.64 GB KV
#   num_ctx 32768 x 4 slots -> ~2.21 GB KV      (+0.57 GB)
#
# Weights are ~7 GB, the card is 16 GB, and qwen2.5-coder:fim (2.73 GB) is the
# other resident tag, so the delta fits comfortably.
#
# 32768 IS VERY LIKELY SUFFICIENT, THOUGH NOT PROVEN. The unknown is the
# CONTINUATION that would have been generated with more room, not a destroyed
# original prompt -- the prompts are recoverable and top out at 13,666 tokens.
# At 32768 those same requests get ~19,000 tokens of generation headroom
# against an overview target of ~3,000, and the largest generation actually
# observed was 7,909 before it hit the wall. That is a lower bound on what the
# model wanted, not an upper bound, so sufficiency stays unproven.
#
# VERIFY AFTER THE RELOAD, with an ABSOLUTE window -- `--since "9 hours ago"`
# slides and will re-count today's 16,383 events:
#
#   journalctl -u ollama --since "<exact post-reload timestamp>" \
#     | grep -o "n_tokens = [0-9]*, truncated = [0-9]*"
#
# A clean result is zero truncated=1. If they reappear they will sit at 32,767,
# and 65,536 costs a FURTHER ~1.14 GB (3.35 GB total KV at 4 slots) -- not the
# "+0.6 GB" an earlier version of this comment claimed, which wrongly repeated
# the 16K->32K delta instead of computing the 32K->64K one.
#
# NOT independently confirmed: `ollama ps` reports 8.74 GB loaded against a
# 7.15 GB tag, but on-disk tag size is not resident weights and the residual
# includes other buffers, so that subtraction does NOT validate the KV figure.
# The deployed allocation is unverified until the daemon's startup allocation
# log is read after the reload.
#
# DO NOT reconcile these numbers against the "4 slots x num_ctx 8192 = 4.5 GiB
# q8_0 KV" line in llama/host/ollama.service.d/homelab.conf -- that figure
# (IMPR-1083) sized a dense model with no sliding window and many more kv
# heads. Both are right for their model. Any future tag change here must
# re-derive KV from ITS OWN /api/show shape and the allocator's padding rules.
PARAMETER num_ctx 32768
PARAMETER temperature 0
PARAMETER top_p 0.95
PARAMETER top_k 64
